---
<QuestionLayout
  title="What errors does a web compatible capability return?"
  description="On Traverse main, web compatible capabilities return runtime.wasm's lifecycle codes and allow one instance at a time. Published 0.16.2 still uses the old codes."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/what-errors-does-a-web-compatible-capability-return.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> on Traverse main, the same codes every other host returns, because <code>runtime.wasm</code> now runs the lifecycle. That landed in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1824">#1824</a> on November 6, 2026, under <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/main/docs/decision-log.md">Decision 128</a>. It is not in a published package yet.</p>

  <h2>The codes on main</h2>
  <p>Web's <code>EmbedderErrorCode</code> now passes the runtime's codes through instead of mapping them to web-only names:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>compatible_instance_active</code>: a second start while an instance is already running.</li>
    <li><code>compatible_instance_not_active</code>: a stop or kill with no matching running instance. This replaces <code>instance_not_found</code> and <code>instance_not_running</code>.</li>
    <li><code>input_not_canonicalizable</code>: start input that RFC 8785 (JCS) cannot represent, per Spec 1722.</li>
    <li><code>invalid_input</code>: a start request the runtime rejects as invalid.</li>
    <li><code>platform_not_supported</code>: the host platform is not in the capability's <code>platforms[]</code> allowlist.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>One instance at a time</h2>
  <p>The old web lifecycle let you start several instances of the same compatible capability. The runtime allows one running instance. A second start fails with <code>compatible_instance_active</code> until you stop or kill the first. A <code>null</code> instance id on stop or kill selects the running one.</p>

  <h2>Where the start input shows up</h2>
  <p>The public API did not grow. The host keeps the runtime's <code>canonical_input</code> for each instance and publishes start as a <code>state_changed</code> event with that text as <code>input</code>, which is where web's platform wrapper already read it. Stop and kill are <code>state_changed</code> with <code>previous_state: "started"</code>.</p>

  <h2>What to do if you use web compatible mode</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Published <code>traverse-embedder-web</code> 0.16.2 still runs the old TypeScript lifecycle. Nothing changes until you upgrade past it.</li>
    <li>When the next release ships #1824, update any code that matches <code>instance_not_found</code> or <code>instance_not_running</code>, and any flow that starts a second instance.</li>
    <li>The web test double follows the same rules, so tests written against it see real runtime behavior.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Swift, Kotlin, and .NET switched in the same PR. The Rust <code>traverse-embedder</code> crate followed on November 9 in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1828">#1828</a> (Decision 129): it now uses the same shared rules and returns the same codes. Like web, it is on main and not in a tagged release yet.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
